house of the dragon comes out august  21st and i've seen people who are either  willing to give the show a try or people  that are really cynical towards the  world of ice and fire and don't want to  watch the show from how much season 8  shattered them which feels bad man but  right away for people in that camp david  benioff and dan weiss the prior genres  of game of thrones aren't involved in  the creative process for house of the  dragon at all so it's a different  creative team with different writers the  two show runners for the show are ryan j  condell and miguel sapochnick for anyone  who isn't familiar with miguel he  directed episodes like hard home wins of  winter in the battle of the bastards so  him being a shore runner to me is great  and is essentially cherry picking the  best talent from game of thrones then on  the flip side i'm more worried about  ryan kandel mainly because this guy has  written rampage and hercules which are  two critically panned movies rampage in  particular is a movie where the rock is  friends with a giant gorilla and they  fight other big animals in a city so  yeah  and trusting him as one of the shriners  in him writing four of the episodes  definitely has me a bit worried this  kind of reminds me of david benioff who  before working on game of thrones made  x-men origins which is one of the worst  marvel movies so hopefully ryan condell  is much better at writing more dramatic  material than blockbuster popcorn movies  then the rest of the writers for the  show are pretty experienced writing  television so it's hard to judge how  well they will do and on the subject for  writing i think one of the most  reassuring aspects is that the source  material for house of the dragon is  based on fire and blood and it's  finished the main issue with the game of  thrones is that george r martin didn't  finish the last two books which led to  david and dan having to match the  quality from the first four seasons for  the later ones which was practically  impossible for house of the dragon  though we don't have to worry about that  because george r martin already wrote  the ending however there is one aspect  about fire and blood in terms of  translation from source material to  screen that can be worrisome which is  the fact that fire and blood is  primarily a telling of events with very  little dialogue and characterization as  a comparison the books for game of  thrones have internal point of view  chapters that are able to thoroughly  develop characters but for fire and  blood  it's mostly just a maester explaining  what happened in the past so what this  means for the tv show is that there will  probably need to be a lot more  characterization and dialogue that isn't  originally in the book because a tv show  needs to have consistent dialogue and  fully realized characters to keep  viewers engaged for game of thrones a  lot of the original dialogue in the  later seasons that david and dan wrote  was terrible and it stood out like a  sore thumb in comparison to george's  dialogue i know there will be different  writers for house of the dragon but  they'll also all have to deliver  consistent high quality dialogue to fill  in the gaps created by the tv show  format so it could either be a glaring  difference or it can perfectly blend in  and you won't notice what's original and  what's new and obviously the action will  be very well executed because miguel  sapochnick is a master at that we'll  just have to wait and see if the  dialogue scenes are as good as game of  thrones looking at some other people  behind the scenes working on house of  the dragon we have the return of rameen  jawadi which is fantastic because that  was the one aspect of game of thrones  that did not drop in quality at all and  if anything he did even better through  the course of the show so i'm really  excited to see him return and i hope he  creates more original work for the show  and they don't just reuse a lot of the  themes from a game of thrones if that  happens then that would be really sad  but i would be surprised if they did  that then i was going to mention the  cinematographer given that the footage  they showed off so far looks great and  atmospheric but for some reason i  physically can't find out who the dp is  on the show but regardless it's hbo and  essentially all of their shows look  outstanding next we have the cast which  also makes me excited matt smith is one  of the leads playing damon targaryen and  he's an actor that can be very likable  charismatic and despite having those  traits he has a lot of range and can  pull off some serious material as well  it's just a little hard to judge him  currently given that he hasn't had the  best luck in picking movies  morbius but there's a lot of past work  that cements him as a good actor then  patty considine as viscera's targaryen i  haven't seen him in a single role that i  disliked and he was a part of the  cornetto trilogy so that makes him  goaded olivia cook is a great up and  coming-ish actor and i loved her work in  sound of metal and thoroughbreds her  eyes in particular feel extremely  expressive and makes her very  sympathetic those are the three main  actors that i've seen a lot before and  there are a few other highlights that  i've seen briefly like graham mctavish  rhysaphon and jefferson hall which for  jefferson hall it's kind of weird that  he's getting casted as a lannister when  he dies in season one of game of thrones  playing hue of the veil so i doubt there  will be any explanation for that i  assume the casting director thought we  wouldn't notice thought we wouldn't  notice  but we did then for the rest of the cast  i'm excited to see how they do one more  great aspect that this show has over  game of thrones is that the overall ip  george has created is a lot more  valuable now going into the first season  of game of thrones the show had a pretty  small budget and the creators were  forced to cut out a lot of content which  also led to issues down the line because  that cut content early on exponentially  blossoms into differing narratives in  regards to cutting characters and plot  lines like as an example the producers  were really close to cutting out rickon  stark which is mind-boggling that that  almost happened on top of the fact that  game of thrones was trying to show an  entire war of the five kings so imagine  if the first season of game of thrones  had the budget of what house of the  dragon had for their first season so  since house of the dragon has a bigger  budget they won't need to make excessive  sacrifices and they won't have to shy  away from certain cool sequences because  of budgetary issues that doesn't mean  they have an infinite budget but the  creators have a lot more freedom in  telling the best story they possibly can  thus upping the potential for the show  but this does bring one minor downside  from that fact which is hopefully they  don't focus too much on action and  actually take time for great dialogue  scenes in character building because  that was one thing the first few seasons  of game of thrones were forced to do  given that they couldn't do a lot of  battles a scene like robert and cersei  talking about their marriage was  improvised by david and dan given that  they didn't have enough scenes for one  of their episodes so because of a bigger  budget it could also lead to some minor  laziness from a creative perspective  since it doesn't force the writers to  dig deep with their characters on a  smaller scale and again this isn't  guaranteed to happen thanks for watching  and i'll probably be covering the show  weekly with episodic reviews  you

